00:20:15,401 --> 00:20:19,480
was in the days before forklifts, it can be rolled, it can be

262
00:20:19,481 --> 00:20:23,540
rolled with two hands, one hand, one finger. It can be turned on a dime.

263
00:20:23,541 --> 00:20:27,320
It can easily turn around. It can be rocked from side to side,

264
00:20:27,321 --> 00:20:31,040
rocking so fast that you can practically get it to jump up and stand on its edge,

265
00:20:31,280 --> 00:20:33,240
and it can be rolled along its edge.

266
00:20:33,440 --> 00:20:35,000
So it's an incredible shape.

267
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:39,340
And it was used in the 19th century, but it's an ancient shape.

268
00:20:39,400 --> 00:20:41,360
It was used all the way back to the time of the Egyptians.

269
00:20:42,920 --> 00:20:45,960
By its very shape, the barrel was a tool unto itself.

270
00:20:46,780 --> 00:20:48,640
Part wheel, part lever.

271
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:55,700
But as amazing as it was, sailors still relied on strong hands and backs to get them on board.

272
00:20:55,701 --> 00:21:01,200
but by the early 19th century the power of steam was beginning to change all that

273
00:21:01,201 --> 00:21:13,100
but the adoption of steam for hoisting mechanisms changes the game to a great degree all of a sudden

274
00:21:13,101 --> 00:21:18,880
you don't have to have seven men to pull on a rope move a bucket a steam boiler and an engine could

275
00:21:18,881 --> 00:21:24,820
do it better and cheaper and faster but steam power was more than just a catalyst for speed

276
00:21:24,821 --> 00:21:30,440
For over a century, dock workers faced both the challenge of mechanization and the right to organize.

277
00:21:32,380 --> 00:21:36,280
The confrontation would lead to some of the bloodiest days on the docks.

278
00:21:37,040 --> 00:21:43,600
On July 5, 1934, near the San Francisco waterfront, police fired on brick-throwing strikers.

279
00:21:46,160 --> 00:21:51,100
Two men were killed and nearly 80 wounded in what would become known as Bloody Thursday.

280
00:21:51,101 --> 00:22:01,060
public outcry against the brutality strengthened the workers cause by the 1950s the longshoremen

281
00:22:01,061 --> 00:22:07,240
had the upper hand and their union had become one of the strongest in the nation those on the water

282
00:22:07,241 --> 00:22:13,180
front became the poster boys for blue collar working America in 1950s you would have teams

283
00:22:13,181 --> 00:22:19,060
of longshoremen eight men in a gang assigned to a hold and so these men in packing the goods had

284
00:22:19,061 --> 00:22:23,100
to work as teams, to get it in there safely and effectively

285
00:22:23,101 --> 00:22:24,820
so it wouldn't shift during the voyage,

286
00:22:24,821 --> 00:22:26,980
and then also work as effectively and safely

287
00:22:26,981 --> 00:22:30,380
to get it out without damaging themselves or the goods.

288
00:22:30,381 --> 00:22:33,260
So it was a great, great deal of camaraderie, teamwork,

289
00:22:33,261 --> 00:22:36,560
a lot of imagination, creativity, right in that hold,

290
00:22:36,561 --> 00:22:38,800
working as gangs.

291
00:22:38,801 --> 00:22:40,960
Despite the close-knit working environment,

292
00:22:40,961 --> 00:22:43,420
the gangs had to remain vigilant.

293
00:22:43,421 --> 00:22:46,400
Working down in a cargo hold could be very dangerous.

294
00:22:46,401 --> 00:22:48,160
If you were breaking out of cargo,

295
00:22:48,161 --> 00:22:52,640
you in fact were relying on the skill of the men who had loaded that cargo somewhere else in the world.

296
00:22:52,980 --> 00:22:57,680
Because a vessel rolls in a seaway, 10 foot, 15 foot, 25 foot seas,

297
00:22:57,980 --> 00:23:02,860
get a vessel rolling and pitching, and those goods could shift if they're not well stowed,

298
00:23:02,980 --> 00:23:05,820
and as you try to break it out, disaster could loom.

299
00:23:07,960 --> 00:23:13,300
But by the late 1970s, the introduction of containerization had changed everything

300
00:23:13,301 --> 00:23:17,040
and resurrected an old rivalry, man versus machine.

301
00:23:17,041 --> 00:23:20,550
In 2002, a West Coast shipping lockout cost the U.S.

302
00:23:20,551 --> 00:23:23,520
economy an estimated $1 to $2 billion a day.

303
00:23:23,820 --> 00:23:27,780
At the heart of the issue was the age-old tumult over jobs lost to mechanization.

304
00:23:28,760 --> 00:23:30,920
But containerization clearly offered benefits.

305
00:23:31,460 --> 00:23:32,919
It did several things.

306
00:23:32,920 --> 00:23:38,160
It certainly sped up the process, where vessels might be days or weeks in port.

307
00:23:38,280 --> 00:23:39,680
Now they spend hours in port.

308
00:23:40,320 --> 00:23:45,460
They can take a two-week voyage and time it down to 15 minutes, moving goods on and off.

309
00:23:45,461 --> 00:23:47,820
it saved money enormously.

310
00:23:48,760 --> 00:23:52,900
But today, many worry that the money saved may have come at the cost of security.

311
00:23:54,220 --> 00:23:58,060
Only 3% of the containers that entered the U.S. are physically inspected,

312
00:23:58,660 --> 00:24:02,060
leaving many lawmakers to wonder what's really coming through the nation's ports.

313
00:24:03,100 --> 00:24:08,240
Congress is attempting to put over a billion dollars into cargo scanners and additional port protection.

314
00:24:09,400 --> 00:24:11,860
Critics contend even that won't be enough to ensure safety.

315
00:24:11,861 --> 00:24:16,280
But with billions at stake, one thing is sure.

316
00:24:16,940 --> 00:24:19,280
These loading docks will have to continue to operate

317
00:24:19,281 --> 00:24:22,920
because they've become an indispensable part of the world's economy.

