00:35:31,874 --> 00:35:34,320
It's one of the most active areas that we have in the building.

451
00:35:34,321 --> 00:35:39,320
Scanners read and record the information off every package's shipping slip.

452
00:35:39,321 --> 00:35:46,320
The information technology allows the computerized conveyor system to move each package to its new loading area,

453
00:35:46,321 --> 00:35:52,320
where workers put them back into containers for the next leg of their travels.

454
00:35:52,321 --> 00:35:55,320
The automation aspect is extremely unique.

455
00:35:55,321 --> 00:36:02,320
Basically, the only human beings that touch the packages are the unload and the load positions.

456
00:36:02,321 --> 00:36:07,140
Once a package is inducted into the facility, everything else is driven off of the smart label,

457
00:36:07,141 --> 00:36:12,100
which means it's electronically read data that guides that package throughout the system

458
00:36:12,101 --> 00:36:15,880
and puts it in the proper container for outbound dispatch.

459
00:36:17,340 --> 00:36:19,220
It wasn't always so easy.

460
00:36:19,840 --> 00:36:22,780
In the early days of air cargo, everything was manual.

461
00:36:23,240 --> 00:36:30,080
Basically, the loading and the sortation of the packages were folks just loading in bags of merchandise

462
00:36:30,081 --> 00:36:33,660
and putting them on prop planes for delivery to their destinations.

463
00:36:34,380 --> 00:36:38,700
As early as 1911, the U.S. Post Office began to offer air mail

464
00:36:38,701 --> 00:36:41,600
and contracted outside companies to fly the planes.

465
00:36:42,620 --> 00:36:46,840
By the late 20s, many were using Douglas M-2 biplanes for the job.

466
00:36:48,220 --> 00:36:50,980
The cargo containers located between the pilot and the engine

467
00:36:50,981 --> 00:36:53,500
held nearly 1,000 pounds of mail.

468
00:36:54,460 --> 00:36:56,200
But as with all planes of the era,

469
00:36:56,760 --> 00:36:59,560
everything had to be painstakingly loaded by hand.

470
00:37:00,080 --> 00:37:10,920
Over the next 15 years, aircraft improvements allowed pilots to carry larger loads more efficiently.

471
00:37:13,960 --> 00:37:20,060
But it wasn't until the onset of World War II that the world began to see the airplane's true transport potential.

472
00:37:21,840 --> 00:37:28,480
With World War II, you now had the need to move millions of tons of supplies in order to support the war effort.

473
00:37:28,481 --> 00:37:35,480
ammunition, food, clothing, everything you can think of that they needed to support an army had to be moved.

474
00:37:35,481 --> 00:37:41,480
The U.S. Air Transport Command was created on June 20, 1942.

475
00:37:41,481 --> 00:37:49,480
Over the course of the war, ATC pilots delivered over one million tons of vital military equipment.

476
00:37:49,481 --> 00:37:54,480
Still, the loading dock work was right out of the Stone Age,

477
00:37:54,481 --> 00:37:57,440
even though the airplanes were on the verge of the jet age.

478
00:38:03,060 --> 00:38:07,180
Jet power allowed airplanes to become truly viable cargo carriers.

479
00:38:08,220 --> 00:38:12,415
One of the first to recognize the jet's cargo-carrying potential was the U.S.

480
00:38:12,416 --> 00:38:13,116
Air Force.

481
00:38:13,920 --> 00:38:17,500
In the early 60s, the Pentagon commissioned the C-141.

482
00:38:18,940 --> 00:38:21,360
The load and dock had been built right into the plane.

483
00:38:21,361 --> 00:38:28,360
An early version of a roller floor system and forklift specially designed for the aircraft

484
00:38:28,361 --> 00:38:32,360
allowed airmen to quickly load pallets containing weapons and supplies.

485
00:38:32,361 --> 00:38:38,360
But the speed and ease of loading paled in comparison to the delivery system.

486
00:38:38,361 --> 00:38:40,360
Parachutes could be attached to everything.

487
00:38:40,361 --> 00:38:46,360
When the C-141 was over the drop site, airmen opened the rear of the plane and pushed everything out.

488
00:38:46,361 --> 00:38:53,360
The receiving dock was anywhere the plane flew.

489
00:38:53,361 --> 00:39:01,360
Today, the military's innovation has become big business's mainstay.

490
00:39:09,360 --> 00:39:16,360
The interplay between modern airplane loading docks and aircraft means nearly anything can be shipped to the ends of the ship.

491
00:39:16,361 --> 00:39:17,980
the earth within hours.

492
00:39:20,760 --> 00:39:22,420
UPS runs the 11th

493
00:39:22,421 --> 00:39:23,900
largest airline in the world.

494
00:39:24,960 --> 00:39:26,260
Loading docks will return on

495
00:39:26,261 --> 00:39:28,360
Modern Marvels.

496
00:39:29,100 --> 00:39:30,360
Now we turn

497
00:39:30,361 --> 00:39:32,320
to loading docks on Modern Marvels.

498
00:39:34,300 --> 00:39:36,660
As factory owners and manufacturers

499
00:39:36,661 --> 00:39:38,300
become more dependent on an

500
00:39:38,301 --> 00:39:39,840
uninterrupted stream of goods,

501
00:39:40,780 --> 00:39:42,140
the line between

502
00:39:42,141 --> 00:39:44,120
loading dock and warehouse becomes less

503
00:39:44,121 --> 00:39:44,821
defined.

504
00:39:46,360 --> 00:39:53,360
Until recently, industry personnel discussed warehouses in terms of size and storage capacity.

505
00:39:53,361 --> 00:39:58,360
Now throughput and supply stream are the buzzwords.

506
00:39:58,361 --> 00:40:05,360
Today, many warehouses are centralized hubs where goods are transferred in an operation known as cross-stocking.

507
00:40:05,361 --> 00:40:10,914
A cross-stock operation is designed to distribute. It's designed to sort.

508
00:40:10,915 --> 00:40:12,360
That's all it does.

509
00:40:12,361 --> 00:40:16,760
It is not designed to warehouse goods for any length of time at all.

510
00:40:16,960 --> 00:40:24,920
As a matter of fact, the efficiency of a cross-stocking operation is measured in a number of pieces that are handled in any given period of time.

511
00:40:25,580 --> 00:40:30,340
To keep the material moving, modern warehouse workers have many tools at their disposal.

512
00:40:31,900 --> 00:40:37,120
Simple winches, dollies, and push carts share the floor with state-of-the-art lift trucks and pallet movers.

513
00:40:37,121 --> 00:40:44,120
warehouse designers are always looking for ways to speed up the process

514
00:40:44,121 --> 00:40:50,600
may have 2 000 cartons or more on the trailer you want to unload them quickly and account for them

515
00:40:50,601 --> 00:40:57,280
so what we see today is that we'll put a conveyor on the dock that actually extends into the trailer

516
00:40:57,281 --> 00:41:04,720
it may even change elevation to make the unloading operation easy but designers use some of the

517
00:41:04,721 --> 00:41:07,940
highest tech gadgetry long before the warehouse is even built.

518
00:41:08,880 --> 00:41:11,220
Computer-aided drafting, or CAD systems,

519
00:41:11,680 --> 00:41:13,760
allow warehouse builders to glimpse the future.

520
00:41:14,480 --> 00:41:17,880
We can use the CAD system to test our design.

521
00:41:18,300 --> 00:41:22,320
It can be exported electronically to a simulation program.

522
00:41:22,321 --> 00:41:26,520
The simulation program allows us to put movement on the system.

523
00:41:27,640 --> 00:41:31,820
We can actually show the product moving on the conveyors.

524
00:41:31,821 --> 00:41:36,820
The designers' goal is to use the computers to troubleshoot any flaws.

525
00:41:36,821 --> 00:41:40,820
But the computers aren't relegated to the design process.

526
00:41:40,821 --> 00:41:47,820
Computerized warehouse and automatic storage and retrieval systems are currently being developed.

527
00:41:47,821 --> 00:41:55,820
Inside these new facilities, computer-controlled vehicles are beginning to do the jobs once performed by humans.

528
00:41:55,821 --> 00:42:02,700
humans. A revolution in technology on the docks is the AGVS or automatic guided

529
00:42:02,701 --> 00:42:07,160
vehicles. You could even call it a robot. There's no man involved. We have the

530
00:42:07,161 --> 00:42:12,260
equivalent of a truck that has the ability to memorize the facility layout.

531
00:42:12,261 --> 00:42:17,180
Sensors on the vehicle interact with stationary beacons or onboard mapping

532
00:42:17,181 --> 00:42:22,160
software to make sure the machine finds its target. The industry's ultimate goal

533
00:42:22,161 --> 00:42:23,540
is the Lights Out facility,

534
00:42:24,500 --> 00:42:26,780
like something out of a science fiction novel.

535
00:42:27,320 --> 00:42:28,900
Inside the warehouse of the future,

536
00:42:29,440 --> 00:42:31,220
the computers and machines will store,

537
00:42:32,020 --> 00:42:33,560
sort, retrieve,

538
00:42:34,100 --> 00:42:37,140
and load goods without human interaction.

539
00:42:37,960 --> 00:42:39,920
I could envision someday

540
00:42:39,921 --> 00:42:42,740
there being a robotic device,

541
00:42:43,300 --> 00:42:44,660
C-Pretio, for example,

542
00:42:44,661 --> 00:42:46,080
that would meet a carton

543
00:42:46,081 --> 00:42:49,260
and have the intelligence to take that item

544
00:42:49,261 --> 00:42:53,140
and find the next available appropriate place in a truck to load it.

545
00:42:53,880 --> 00:42:56,340
That would be my vision. Are we close to that today?

546
00:42:56,900 --> 00:43:00,220
I think with enough money and energy, we could probably build a system,

547
00:43:00,360 --> 00:43:03,520
but is it cause justified? Probably not today.

548
00:43:04,760 --> 00:43:08,660
The term lights out is literal. Machines don't need light to see.

549
00:43:09,600 --> 00:43:13,480
Unlike the loading docks of old, which were the sole domain of hardworking laborers,

550
00:43:13,920 --> 00:43:17,660
the loading docks of the future won't even be visible to the human eye.

551
00:43:19,260 --> 00:43:24,260
at least the ones here on Earth.

552
00:43:24,261 --> 00:43:28,260
Even astronauts depend on loading docks.

553
00:43:28,261 --> 00:43:41,260
To live in space, every necessity must be delivered by rocket.

554
00:43:41,261 --> 00:43:45,260
Despite the high-tech delivery system and nature of space stations,

555
00:43:45,261 --> 00:43:49,940
Astronauts, like many dock workers on Earth, must unload most of the cargo by hand.

556
00:43:51,620 --> 00:43:54,480
Of course, it's much easier when the packages weigh nothing.

557
00:43:55,620 --> 00:44:00,320
The biggest problem for an astronaut is maneuvering the packages within the confined spaces

558
00:44:00,321 --> 00:44:02,260
and keeping everything under control.

559
00:44:02,820 --> 00:44:06,380
The best system thus far is a space-age version of the bucket brigade.

560
00:44:08,220 --> 00:44:13,080
Astronauts position themselves along the line of transfer and toss the cargo to one another.

561
00:44:13,081 --> 00:44:22,640
In a strange backward step, the pinnacle of high-tech loading docks relies on the earliest form of technology, human muscle.

